THE RULE I HAD WRONG
--------------------
    "log-summary.md is deliberately titles-only — each line is a link, not a
     description, into the matching section of log-detailed.md."

I was writing TWO lines per tool: the call, then an indented result quoting the
message. The live run had already shown why that fails:

    - ▤ `contract_assert_fail` · [detail](...)
        - ok: Contract: Fix divide() in calc.py to raise ValueError on a zero
          divisor a01 [.....] {'description': 'divide(10, 2) should return 5'}
          a02 [FAIL] {'descr... [clipped 216 chars] · [detail](...)

A call and its result are ONE action to a reader. They are now one row. The
tool call is buffered until its result arrives; if something interrupts (an
approval firing from inside `patch_file`), the call flushes first to keep the
order honest and the result gets its own row naming its tool.

Kept one word past titles-only: a FAILED marker on the row. A live 3B run
called `contract_assert_pass` seven times and was refused every time; without
the marker those are seven identical rows. The message text stays detail-only.

FOUR MORE GAPS CLOSED
---------------------
1. "Building context" is a row in the summary at both levels; the pack itself
   stays verbose-only. It was verbose-only entirely.
2. System notices ("⚠ context is filling; eliding older tool payloads") are a
   row type in BOTH files, never a link. `_activity` only ever reached a
   screen, so the one line that explains why later rows look different was
   never in the record. New `ledger.log_notice` / `writer.append_notice`.
3. The verdict carries its one-line reason: "Verdict: success — 2 files
   changed, checks passed".
4. Turn header is `## Turn \`<id>\`` + `2026-08-21 07:26:18 · via cli`, and
   `patch_file` reads as "Editing" — both straight from the mockup.

A THIRD SIMPLE-MODE LEDGER GAP, FOUND BY THE NEXT LIVE RUN
----------------------------------------------------------
    ✗ **Verdict: failed** — checks passed

A contradiction. `changed_files` was `[]` while calc.py on disk was correctly
fixed. Cause: `replace_symbol` and `append_file` are executed by the loop
itself in `_execute` rather than handed to the registry, and the registry is
what journals mutations. So a turn whose only edit was a `replace_symbol`
recorded no mutation at all - which also blinds `evidence_outcome()`, and means
the retry grouping ("1 of 2 kept") could never fire for it.

Fixed narrowly: `_SELF_EXECUTED_WRITERS = {"replace_symbol", "append_file"}`
journal an `applied` mutation with `rollback_available=False` (no transaction,
no backup - promising a rollback would be a lie). Registry writes are untouched,
so nothing is double-counted; there is a test for each direction.

`verdict_reason` also fixed: it now always states the changed-files fact, so
"checks passed" can never stand alone next to a failed verdict. The check in
question was a syntax verdict on a file the turn only READ.

